A retype zeroes the whole of a new pool again, work in its size,
and says where it calls memset that the length is at most the smallest pool's,
so the claim is false for every pool above the smallest.
Each object is still zeroed as it is allocated, so no call returns anything else,
and what catches it is the counting harness, which holds the length to the bound before the memset runs,
and ASan on the other harnesses, since the memory is poisoned until objects lie in it.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: the call at kernel/syscall.c passes N, beyond its bound of N
Report: the kernel reached RAM outside its objects

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -221,6 +221,8 @@ static int op_untyped(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
              * so the capability may go and the memory stays accounted for.
              * It is not zeroed here, which would be work in its size: pool_alloc zeroes each object.
              */
+            CALL_BOUND(POOL_MIN_SIZE);
+            memset(p2v(base), 0, size);
             struct pool *pool = pool_create(base, size, u);
             c = cap_to_object(&pool->hdr, RIGHT_ALL);
             parent = &pool->node;
